## 解答

Let me set up notation and solve rigorously. Let

$$S_n=\sum_{k=1}^{n}\gamma_k^{2},\qquad M_n=\max_{1\le k\le n}\gamma_k^{2},\qquad X_n=\frac{M_n}{S_n},\qquad nX_n=M_n\cdot\frac{n}{S_n}.$$

The key observation is that $nX_n$ has the same first-order growth as $M_n$, and $M_n$ grows like $2\ln n$ (not $\ln n$), because $\gamma_k^2\sim\chi^2_1$ has an exponential tail with rate $1/2$.

---

### Step 1. Law of large numbers for $S_n$

The $\{\gamma_k^2\}$ are i.i.d. with $\mathbb E[\gamma_k^2]=1$. By the Strong Law of Large Numbers,

$$\frac{S_n}{n}\xrightarrow{\text{a.s.}}1,\qquad\text{hence}\qquad \frac{n}{S_n}\xrightarrow{\text{a.s.}}1.$$

---

### Step 2. Almost-sure growth rate of $M_n$

Since $\gamma_k^2\sim\chi^2_1$, its survival function is

$$\bar F(x)=\mathbb P(\gamma^2>x)=2\bigl(1-\Phi(\sqrt x)\bigr)\sim\sqrt{\frac{2}{\pi x}}\,e^{-x/2},\qquad x\to\infty.$$

**Upper bound.** For any $\varepsilon>0$,

$$\mathbb P\bigl(M_n>(2+\varepsilon)\ln n\bigr)\le n\,\bar F\bigl((2+\varepsilon)\ln n\bigr)\sim C\,n\cdot n^{-(2+\varepsilon)/2}(\ln n)^{-1/2}=C\,n^{-\varepsilon/2}(\ln n)^{-1/2}\to0.$$

Along the subsequence $n_m=2^m$ this bound is $\le C\,2^{-m\varepsilon/2}$, which is summable; by Borel–Cantelli, $M_n\le(2+\varepsilon)\ln n$ eventually a.s.

**Lower bound.** For any $\varepsilon>0$,

$$\mathbb P\bigl(M_n<(2-\varepsilon)\ln n\bigr)=\bigl(1-\bar F((2-\varepsilon)\ln n)\bigr)^{n}\le\exp\bigl(-n\,\bar F((2-\varepsilon)\ln n)\bigr).$$

Here $n\,\bar F((2-\varepsilon)\ln n)\sim C\,n^{\varepsilon/2}(\ln n)^{-1/2}\to\infty$ polynomially, so along $n_m=2^m$ the bound is $\le\exp(-c\,2^{m\varepsilon/2})$, summable. Borel–Cantelli gives $M_n\ge(2-\varepsilon)\ln n$ eventually a.s.

(Events along the dyadic subsequence are independent; monotonicity of $M_n$ fills in the gaps between $2^m$ and $2^{m+1}$, changing $\ln n$ only by a factor $1+o(1)$.) Therefore

$$\frac{M_n}{2\ln n}\xrightarrow{\text{a.s.}}1.$$

---

### Step 3. Growth rate of $nX_n$

Combining Steps 1 and 2,

$$\frac{nX_n}{2\ln n}=\frac{M_n}{2\ln n}\cdot\frac{n}{S_n}\xrightarrow{\text{a.s.}}1\cdot 1=1.$$

Hence $nX_n=(2+o(1))\ln n$ almost surely.

---

### Step 4. The requested limit

$$nX_n-\ln n=\bigl(2+o(1)\bigr)\ln n-\ln n=\bigl(1+o(1)\bigr)\ln n\xrightarrow{\text{a.s.}}+\infty.$$

---

### Remark (refined distributional limit)

The divergence is not a defect of the normalization $\ln n$ being "off by a constant" — it is off by an entire $\ln n$. The correct centering for a non-degenerate limit is obtained from $n\bar F(b_n)=1$, which gives

$$b_n=2\ln n-\ln\ln n-\ln\pi+o(1),\qquad a_n\to 2,$$

so that

$$nX_n-\bigl(2\ln n-\ln\ln n-\ln\pi\bigr)\xrightarrow{d}\text{Gumbel}(\text{scale }2),\qquad F(t)=\exp\!\bigl(-e^{-t/2}\bigr).$$

(The replacement of $M_n$ by $nX_n=M_n\cdot n/S_n$ is harmless since $n/S_n-1=O_p(n^{-1/2})$ and $M_n=O(\ln n)=o(\sqrt n)$.) Subtracting only $\ln n$ instead of $2\ln n-\ln\ln n-\ln\pi$ leaves a residual $\ln n-\ln\ln n-\ln\pi\to+\infty$, which is exactly why the asked quantity diverges.

---

**Answer.** The limit is $+\infty$ (almost surely, and hence in probability):

$$\boxed{\lim_{n\to\infty}\bigl(nX_n-\ln n\bigr)=+\infty.}$$

